The code statements that lead to the bug are:
rh_reg_write(RING_CONTROL, RING_CTRL_ABORT);
wait_for_completion_timeout(&rh->op_done, HZ);
Reason:
The original code unconditionally writes only RING_CTRL_ABORT, which clears RING_CTRL_ENABLE, resetting hardware ring pointers even if the ring is already stopped. It also waits on the completion without reinitializing it, causing incorrect wait behavior (e.g., returning immediately if the completion was already signaled from a previous abort). Additionally, it does not check RING_STATUS_RUNNING before aborting, leading to spurious warnings and potential disruption when the ring is not actually running.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
ring_status = rh_reg_read(RING_STATUS);
if (ring_status & RING_STATUS_RUNNING) {
    reinit_completion(&rh->op_done);
    rh_reg_write(RING_CONTROL, RING_CTRL_ENABLE | RING_CTRL_ABORT);
    wait_for_completion_timeout(&rh->op_done, HZ);
    ring_status = rh_reg_read(RING_STATUS);
    if (ring_status & RING_STATUS_RUNNING) {
        dev_crit(&hci->master.dev, "unable to abort the ring\n");
        WARN_ON(1);
    }
}
Reason:
The fix first checks whether the ring is running; if not, it skips the abort entirely, treating the stopped ring as success. If running, it reinitializes the completion to ensure a fresh wait, then writes both RING_CTRL_ENABLE and RING_CTRL_ABORT together so that the enable bit remains set, preventing hardware pointer reset. After waiting, it rechecks the running status to confirm the abort succeeded, and only warns if it fails. This corrects all four flaws in the original logic.

